// ui.cla: Toolbox extern trap block for the ported UI runtime, "slice A"
// (native-5e Task 7, Step 1 ONLY -- no bodies, no `overlay record`s yet;
// those land in Step 2+). Ports runtime/mac/rt_ui.c function-by-function
// into runtime/clarus/ui*.cla, the same way text.cla/list.cla/map.cla
// ported rt_core.inc -- but UI has no host/console equivalent (there is no
// text-mode window/menu/event system), so this module is Mac-ONLY: its
// externs get a matching rt_ext_Ui* wrapper in runtime/mac/rt_ext_mac.inc
// ONLY, never in runtime/host/rt_ext_host.inc (contrast text.cla,
// whose externs get a wrapper on BOTH sides).
//
// Scope (Task 7 brief's slice A, union of rt_ui.c line ranges): 334-926,
// 1175-1213, 2122-2384, 2483-2537, 2623-2758, 2760-3141, 3421-3751,
// 3806-3816+3919-3962 (rt_ui_launch's signature + its RT_MAC_TEST branch
// ONLY -- the real-build AppleEvent branch, :3963-4028, plus the AE
// handlers themselves, rt_ui_ae_check_missed/ae_oapp/ae_odoc/ae_pdoc/
// ae_quit at :3817-3918, are OUT of scope for this step: AE/Resource/
// AppFiles/WD machinery needs real Pascal function-pointer callbacks,
// which Clarus cannot express -- a later step keeps that whole mechanism
// as C-side glue in rt_ext_mac.inc, reachable from ported Clarus through a
// small number of new glue externs THAT step adds itself, not these),
// 4043-4464, 4467-4914, 5294-5337, 5339-5723. Two exclusion rules apply
// throughout: (1) no List-Manager/TextEdit-Manager call (LDispose,
// LActivate, LSetSelect, LUpdate, TEDispose, TEActivate, TEDeactivate,
// TEClick, TEKey, TECalText, TESetSelect, TESetText, TETextBox, TEUpdate)
// gets an extern here -- those only appear in code paths for the
// field/textview/table widget kinds, out of scope this slice; wherever one
// appears inside an otherwise in-scope function only because of a
// kind-switch arm for an out-of-scope kind, that whole arm is a later
// task's problem. (2) Ch10 forms (rt_ui_edit and its own helpers --
// rt_ui_form_teardown/form_cancel/parse_int/parse_fixed/form_invalid/
// form_fill/form_accept/form_is_new, rt_ui.c:4915-5293) are, for the same
// reason, excluded even though their numeric lines fall inside the outer
// 4467-5337 span the brief cites: a form only ever binds popup/field/table
// widgets (out of scope), rtUiEdit is absent from this task's own Produces
// list, and its one Toolbox call NumToString never appears outside that
// span -- so it needed no extern here at all (confirmed by grep: every
// NumToString call site in rt_ui.c falls either inside this excluded span
// or inside the also-out-of-scope rt_ui_fixed_to_str/rt_ui_table_draw_field
// helpers, :1048-1130).
//
// `Ui`-prefixed extern names (UiSetPort, UiFrontWindow, ...), never the
// bare Toolbox name: same collision hazard text.cla's own header comment
// documents for Text/List/Map/Ser's own prefixed copies of NewPtr/
// DisposeHandle/etc -- checkFuncSig/scopeDeclare key purely on NAME (no
// signature comparison), so a user program's own `external func SetPort`
// (the documented Ch13 escape hatch) would otherwise collide with this
// module's. Every Pascal-string-buffer parameter (a menu/window/control
// title, an itemText out-buffer, DrawString's text, ParamText's four
// strings, ...) is declared `ptr`, NOT `str`: Clarus has no str->ptr
// conversion and no address-of for a local `str` variable, but Step 2's
// callers need to pass BOTH blob-computed addresses (already `ptr`,
// pointing into the UI descriptor blob's string pool) and runtime-built
// scratch Pascal strings (a `UiNewPtr` scratch + `pokeb`, the same idiom
// text.cla/list.cla already use) to these same traps -- a `str`-typed
// param could accept neither. No existing runtime module declares a `str`
// extern param anywhere (grep confirms); `ptr` is the established idiom.
//
// Every 16-bit Toolbox INTEGER (window/control coordinates, short counts,
// short IDs/refnums/indices, WindowPartCode/ControlPartCode/MenuID/
// EventMask) is `word` (Task 4's extern type, docs/clarus-language-
// reference.md's own worked examples `UiMoveTo(h: word, v: word) = trap
// 0xA893` and `UiFindWindow(pt: int, wpOut: ptr): word = trap 0xA92C` are
// used verbatim below). A 4-byte Toolbox by-value struct (a Point argument
// -- MenuSelect/DragWindow/GrowWindow/TrackBox/TrackGoAway/PtInRect/
// FindWindow's own `pt`) is `int` (packed v/h, v in the high word, matching
// QuickDraw's own Point-as-long convention) -- never a `ptr`, since it's
// passed BY VALUE, not by address. A genuine 32-bit Toolbox value (Size,
// OSType/ResType/FourCharCode, a `long` refcon/result) is `int`. Pointers
// to Rect/EventRecord/WindowRecord/other records are plain `ptr` --
// `overlay record` views of them are a Step 2 concern, not this one.
//
// Trap convention: an ordinary Toolbox call (every Window/Menu/Control/
// QuickDraw/Event/Dialog/Gestalt/OS-Utility routine below, the vast
// majority) is PASCAL convention, `= trap 0xNNNN` with no `reg` suffix.
// The register-based Memory Manager family (UiHLock/UiHUnlock/UiNewPtr/
// UiNewPtrClear/UiNewHandleClear/UiDisposeHandle/UiDisposePtr/
// UiBlockMoveData) mirrors text.cla's own OWN-prefixed copies of the exact
// same primitives byte-for-byte (same trap words, same `reg` clause) --
// same "each module gets its own name" rationale as text.cla's header
// comment, since this module needs its OWN scratch-allocation surface
// distinct from Text/List/Map/Ser/Str/Native's. UiTickCount is the one
// non-Memory-Manager `reg` trap: Inside Macintosh documents it as a
// register-based OS trap (result in D0, no stack args), matching this
// task's own brief. Every trap word below not already in text.cla's own
// verified set was independently re-derived against Retro68's Traps.h
// (converted from CR-only line endings per the repo's own documented
// recipe) AND the actual Universal Interfaces C prototype (MacWindows.h/
// Menus.h/Controls.h/QuickDraw.h/QuickdrawText.h/Events.h/Gestalt.h/
// Dialogs.h/Devices.h -- also CR-only, also converted) for parameter
// order/type, since Traps.h alone gives no parameter information. Four
// trap words this task resolved beyond the brief's own pre-resolved table
// (all confirmed against the real headers, `grep -a` -- these files carry
// the ISO-8859 copyright byte, which makes a locale-default `grep` treat
// them as binary and silently match nothing without `-a`): EqualRect
// 0xA8A6 (QuickDraw.h, aliased `MacEqualRect`), HideCursor 0xA852
// (QuickDraw.h), BeginUpdate/EndUpdate 0xA922/0xA923 (MacWindows.h; the
// Traps.h enum itself spells these `_BeginUpDate`/`_EndUpDate`, capital D,
// but the actual EXTERN_API function names -- and rt_ui.c's own call
// spelling -- are the conventional `BeginUpdate`/`EndUpdate`), SetPortBits
// 0xA875 (QuickDraw.h; the same trap Traps.h's enum calls `_SetPBits` --
// SetPortBits is the modern Universal-Interfaces name for the identical
// trap). BlockMoveData is 0xA22E, NOT 0xA02E (plain BlockMove, a distinct
// trap) -- same distinction text.cla's own header comment makes; verified
// again here directly against QuickDraw.h's own `BlockMoveData` prototype.
//
// Gestalt (UiGestaltErr/UiGestaltValue, below) is a REGISTER trap whose
// raw contract answers in TWO registers: the OSErr in D0 and the RESPONSE
// VALUE in A0. Gestalt.h's `#pragma parameter __D0 Gestalt(__D0, __A1)`
// describes the INLINE GLUE, not the trap -- the glue's second word,
// `TWOWORDINLINE(0xA1AD, 0x2288)`, decodes to `MOVE.L A0,(A1)`, i.e. it
// copies A0 through the caller's `long *response` afterwards. A `reg`
// extern names exactly ONE result register and cannot emit that store, so
// the trap is declared twice -- see toolbox/osutils.cla's own header
// comment for the full decode and the on-hardware proof.
//
// This corrects a long-lived misreading: until pack3-standardfile Task 5a
// this file declared `UiGestalt(selector, response) = trap 0xA1AD
// reg(d0: selector, a1: response) ret d0`, which bound A1 as an unread
// INPUT and dropped A0 entirely, so `*response` was NEVER written on the
// native lane. `err` still came back a clean noErr, so rtUiStartup below
// read uninitialized heap garbage out of its own freshly-UiNewPtr'd
// response slot -- which is exactly the symptom the (now removed)
// `resp < 0x1000` upper bound there was added to paper over. The cprint
// lane was unaffected: its wrapper called the real C `Gestalt()`, glue
// word included.
//
// LMGetCurApName (rt_ui_apple_select's else-branch, rt_ui.c:2295, for a
// program with no `app name` section) is NOT a trap at all -- it is a
// direct read of the CurApName low-memory global at the fixed address
// 0x0910 (Universal Interfaces' own LMGetCurApName is just `(StringPtr)
// 0x910`, a cast of a constant, not a real call). No extern is declared
// for it here; a later step passes `ptr(0x910)` directly to UiParamText's
// first argument at that one call site, using the existing `ptr(intExpr)`
// conversion -- no new language feature needed.
//
// Split from uiwidgets.cla: this file holds Window Manager, Menu Manager,
// Event Manager, Gestalt/OS-Utility, Dialog Manager (Alert family), the
// two universal QuickDraw port primitives (SetPort/GetPort -- read by
// nearly every in-scope function under rt_ui.h's own PORT DISCIPLINE RULE,
// so they belong with the "core" file, not the widget one), and this
// module's own Memory Manager scratch-allocation family -- the traps
// rt_ui_startup/rt_ui_run/window-management/menu-management/every-pump/
// winst-instance-management code needs. uiwidgets.cla holds Control
// Manager basics and QuickDraw drawing/geometry traps -- the ones widget
// layout/creation/canvas-drawing code needs, plus the canvas offscreen
// GrafPort's own OpenPort/ClosePort/PortSize/SetOrigin/SetPortBits (Ch11,
// rt_ui_canvas_make/dispose) since those are canvas-specific, not general
// port save/restore.

// ---- Memory Manager (this module's own scratch-allocation family) ----
// Mirrors text.cla's TextHandleDeref/TextNewPtr/TextDisposePtr/
// TextNewHandle/TextDisposeHandle/TextHLock/TextHUnlock/TextBlockMoveData
// exactly (same trap words, same `reg` clauses) -- this module's own copy,
// per the naming-collision rationale above. No `UiRcCheck`/ARC pairing:
// unlike Text/List/Map, nothing in this slice's winst/canvas allocation is
// reference-counted (rt_ui.c's own struct rt_ui_winst is a plain malloc'd
// -- NewHandleClear'd -- box, freed unconditionally by rt_ui_close, never
// rc-tracked).
// AE handler catalog (mac-resident-clarusc Task 12): pull the real
// AppleEvent Manager declarations from the shared catalog instead of
// hand-declaring them here -- same "catalog-first" convention
// uidialogs.cla's own header comment establishes for toolbox/
// standardfile.cla/files.cla. Resolved relative to this file, i.e.
// <rtdir>/../../toolbox/ -- a user program that also composes
// toolbox/appleevents.cla positionally dedups via the normalized
// include-once identity. Harmless on the cprint lane (conv 9's `seld0`
// clause is invisible to cprint.cla, which never consults a trap/reg/sel
// clause at all -- every extern call renders as `rt_ext_<Name>` uniformly
// regardless), and unreachable there in practice anyway: nat_UiLaunchReal
// (below, near UiLaunchReal) is a clause-less nat_ fallback, resolved by
// cgCallExtNatFallback on the NATIVE lane only; the cprint lane keeps its
// own separate, already-complete rt_ext_UiLaunchReal (rt_ext_mac.inc),
// untouched by this task. Must precede every other declaration in this
// file (`include must precede other declarations`), hence all the way up
// here rather than down by its own point of use.
include "../../toolbox/appleevents.cla"

external func UiHandleDeref(h: ptr): ptr = inline deref
external func UiNewPtrRaw(size: int): ptr = trap 0xA11E reg

// UiNewPtr: the allocation-waist wrapper around UiNewPtrRaw
// (heap-jiggle-stress-mode phase, correctness-cleanup Task 3). Every
// existing call site in this runtime already calls `UiNewPtr` -- this
// wrapper is the smallest diff that hooks the allocation waist without
// touching any of them: `rtUiJiggleTick()` (uiscript.cla) is a no-op
// unless scripted `jiggle on` is in effect, so this is byte-identical
// to a plain trap call by default. Within-handler staleness (a master
// pointer cached before an allocation that can compact the heap, then
// dereferenced after) is the historical bug shape; dispatch-boundary
// jiggle alone (rtUiRunScripted) cannot catch it -- only hooking the
// allocation waist itself does.
func UiNewPtr(size: int): ptr {
    rtUiJiggleTick()
    return UiNewPtrRaw(size)
}

external func UiNewPtrClear(size: int): ptr = trap 0xA31E reg
external func UiNewHandleClear(size: int): ptr = trap 0xA322 reg
external func UiDisposeHandle(h: ptr) = trap 0xA023 reg
external func UiDisposePtr(p: ptr) = trap 0xA01F reg
external func UiHLock(h: ptr) = trap 0xA029 reg
external func UiHUnlock(h: ptr) = trap 0xA02A reg
external func UiBlockMoveData(src: ptr, dst: ptr, count: int) = trap 0xA22E reg

// ---- QuickDraw port basics (PORT DISCIPLINE RULE, rt_ui.h) ----
// Read/written by nearly every in-scope function that draws (each
// self-asserts GetPort/SetPort, restores the caller's port before
// returning) -- too pervasive to belong to either "core" or "widgets"
// alone; kept here since rt_ui_startup itself (the module's own entry
// point) needs GetPort/SetPort before anything widget-shaped exists.
external func UiGetPort(port: ptr) = trap 0xA874
external func UiSetPort(port: ptr) = trap 0xA873

